component SigilSemanticKernel {
  goal {
    Compute consequences of asserted Sigil facts with a stable egglog kernel.
  }
  interface {
    SemanticCompilation {
      Parse ordinary Turtle using N3, normalize RDF terms, validate the versioned
      ontology, lower typed fixed tables, and execute real egglog to closure.
      Return deterministic red, yellow, or green with derivation witnesses.
    }
  }
}

expand SigilSemanticKernel {
  constraints {
    SemanticAuthority {
      Turtle exchanges assertions. Egglog owns inference, numerical computation,
      invariants, and obligations. Unknown vocabulary, generated laws, named graphs,
      and RDF triple terms are rejected. Source metadata remains outside ontology.
      Derived consequences are never silently copied into asserted state.
      Every native output table is required and checked for scalar type and arity.
      IPC has a fixed byte limit. Timeout, cancellation, and I/O failure terminate
      and reap the native process before returning.

      Implementation observation and completeness tables are populated by trusted
      host collectors. Model-proposed Evidence entities cannot establish coverage.
      Negative obligations require a complete observation scope.
    }
  }
  cases {
    SemanticCompilation {
      Closure derives delegated capabilities, dependency reachability, exclusive
      ownership conflicts, required contracts, minimum path cost, maximum reachable
      risk, and implementation coverage. Missing required information is yellow.
      Contradictions and hard violations are red. A structured contract has one
      subject, relation and target; a dependency has one source and destination;
      a numerical property has one value. A matching alternative cannot hide an
      invalid multi-valued contract. Engine failure rejects.
    }
  }
}

component SigilSemanticState {
  goal {
    Persist asserted semantic worlds without treating cached results as proof.
  }
  interface {
    SemanticState {
      Store canonical assertions by content fingerprint and atomically replace a
      revision containing the world fingerprint, source fingerprint, and component
      bindings. Writes compare the expected previous revision under a workspace lock.
      Reads parse assertions again and verify their fingerprint. Content-addressed
      snapshots are immutable. Prepare new snapshots before atomic publication and
      release the write lock even when stale state or cleanup fails.
      New accepted state is a committed immutable bundle in .sigil/world, selected
      by an atomic revision pointer. Store lossless canonical egglog assertions,
      with Turtle available as interchange. Include source and binding metadata in the
      revision identity. Read legacy semantic.json/worlds state during migration.
    }
  }
}

component SigilCompileArtifacts {
  goal {
    Retain incremental compilation inputs, completed stages and witnesses in the
    target codebase without confusing persisted claims with verification authority.
  }
  interface {
    ArtifactBundles {
      Initialize .sigil/world, handoffs, receipts, runs and cache with scoped Git
      ignore rules. Accepted world revisions and verifier policy are committed;
      receipts and operational artifacts are ignored. Preserve existing ignore
      entries. Each immutable bundle contains a versioned manifest, dependency
      fingerprints and hashed payloads. Identical bundles reuse their identity.
      Reject corrupt content and unsafe paths. Publish complete directories before
      mutable heads and compare complete revisions under operating-system locks.

      Compile records completed semantic stages and final reports independently.
      Slice export retains a focused assertion bundle and its obligations, source,
      world and kernel identities. These exports precede the complete retained
      handoff protocol that freezes policy and test oracles for verification.
      Native closure identifies its actual compiled kernel and dependency revision.
      Reopening an artifact verifies its bytes but never restores trusted evidence
      or a proof status. Receipt ingestion and tool execution retain their separate
      authority boundaries. Exclude generated artifacts from source discovery.
      Native egglog parsing accepts only fixed assertion forms with literal string
      arguments. Preserve resource identities, literal datatypes and language tags.
      Project world files cannot define or execute rules, includes or schedules.
    }
  }
}

component SigilSemanticProfile {
  goal {
    Select deterministic stage dependency closures and validated execution budgets.
  }
  interface {
    SemanticProfile {
      standard and critical-system run deterministic-foundation, semantic-closure,
      and implementation-coverage. Custom profiles may extend either base.
      Selected required laws cannot be disabled. No evaluator binding is required.

      Legacy semantic-readiness, architecture-design, and standards-risk stage names
      alias semantic-closure. current-code-compatibility aliases implementation-coverage.
      The effective-profile fingerprint includes the semantic kernel version.
    }
  }
}

component SigilImplementationHandoff {
  goal {
    Preserve exactly the accepted assignment and host verification policy before
    an external coding agent implements it, without scheduling that agent.
  }
  interface {
    RetainedHandoff {
      Retain the complete accepted world, scoped assertions, all derived boundary
      obligations and fact-to-obligation provenance in a content-addressed bundle.
      The caller retains its identity independently of the returned submission.
      Bind source, baseline code, kernel, TypeScript analyzer, component inventory,
      API catalog, required checks and protected specification/configuration/test
      fingerprints. Exact host-selected test files remain protected oracles.

      Reload through the restricted egglog parser and recompute design closure and
      obligations. Reject unknown versions, altered payloads, stale kernels and
      mismatched scope or obligation identities. Saved green is never proof.
      Detect changed or added protected configuration and specification files in
      returned snapshots. Code changes are expected; protected input drift prevents
      certification. Snapshot and tool bounds remain operational failures.
    }
    ReceiptSubmission {
      Parse ordinary Turtle Evidence claims plus a strict source-location sidecar
      referring to the caller-retained handoff identity. Resolve covers references
      to exported obligation or fact identities without reducing the required set.
      Require exact proposition agreement and reject unknown or conflicting claims.
      Keep submitted assertions and source selectors untrusted. File hashes and
      native symbol resolution must be checked independently before witness joins.
    }
  }
}

component SigilSemanticIntentSearch {
  goal {
    Turn natural-language requests into candidate Turtle worlds and preserve
    consequential ambiguity until an exact intent answer distinguishes the worlds.
  }
  interface {
    IntentSearch {
      Record each request as a protected required contract before generation.
      A SemanticProposalProvider returns only untrusted Turtle candidate patches.
      SigilSemanticKernel computes closure and deterministic ranking selects viable
      worlds. Unambiguous intent requests one candidate; consequential ambiguity may
      produce a bounded beam. Empty patches leave new required intent unresolved.

      For tied worlds, compute the exact discriminating proposition and optionally
      ask the provider to phrase it as a question. Always retain the exact proposition
      alongside model wording; the renderer cannot choose a world or change fact identity.
    }
    BeamPersistence {
      Store asserted base Turtle, candidate Turtle deltas, authorized mutable facts,
      and exact intent answers. Validate the checkpoint schema and bound its size.
      Atomic revision-checked writes preserve concurrent edits. Replay recomputes
      closure and ranking and never accepts persisted proof or status.
    }
    CommandProposalProvider {
      Send the proposal prompt over stdin to a caller-selected executable and accept
      a JSON transport envelope containing ordinary Turtle patches on stdout.
      Reuse bounded adapter lifecycle and cleanup. Run in a disposable directory;
      provide the semantic context in the prompt instead of a writable checkout.
    }
  }
}

component SigilImplementationEvidence {
  goal {
    Collect deterministic observations from implementation tools while preserving
    the difference between observed source structure and behavioral proof.
  }
  interface {
    EvidenceCollection {
      A host-owned implementation policy selects a TypeScript project, exact
      component file inventories and bindings to modules, declarations or globals.
      TypeScript 7 resolves static direct dependencies and call targets. Each
      observation produces ordinary Turtle plus a source-range and input-hash
      receipt outside the semantic ontology. Evidence stays separate from design
      assertions. Aliases resolve to their exported symbols and indirect callable
      parameters remain opaque. Inventories never imply runtime execution.

      A passing native typecheck satisfies only its mandatory tool check. Complete
      negative dependency scopes require an explicit exhaustive inventory, every
      file present, no errors and no opaque calls or unresolved imports. Calls,
      arbitrary behavior and API effects do not receive absence certificates.
    }
    TypeScriptAnalysis {
      Use the pinned TypeScript 7 native compiler API to obtain syntax and type
      diagnostics, AST import references, symbol-resolved call declarations,
      lexical global references, and opaque dynamic behavior from one project
      snapshot. Fingerprint source content and effective compiler options.

      TypeScript analysis is read-only. Timeout, cancellation, or protocol failure
      terminates and reaps the native process. Version-specific API and cleanup
      details remain isolated in the TypeScript adapter.
    }
    MechanicalCoverage {
      A collector's completeness scope may identify one semantic target. It cannot
      close a different target's negative obligation. Host-selected tool checks
      require actual host results; failed checks are hard implementation violations.
      Proposed Evidence assertions cannot stand in for these trusted tool results.
      Bounded host compiler and test commands return actual exit codes and output
      hashes. A failed tool command is evidence; launch, timeout, cancellation and
      output-limit failures remain operational failures. Commands execute inside
      disposable implementation snapshots with frozen policy and test oracles.
    }
  }
}
